---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseSection from "../../components/CaseSection.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import Glance from "../../components/Glance.astro";
---

<CaseStudy
  slug="itonics-web-clipper"
  team="1 PM & 5 engineers (front-end & back-end)"
>
  <Glance>
    <Fragment slot="challenge">
      The clipping process required a lot of manual work — including specifying a
      storage destination for every entry — which was time-consuming and
      inefficient, limiting app usage and engagement.
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="strategy">
      Streamline the workflow with clearer navigation, smarter pre-fills, bulk
      actions, and more flexible capture — shaped through a stakeholder workshop
      and user research.
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="impact">
      4 of 5 users found the improved process straightforward and comprehensible;
      the redesign moved into implementation, targeting Chrome and Firefox.
    </Fragment>
  </Glance>

  <CaseSection num="01" title="Context">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        ITONICS Web Clipper is a browser extension that helps users quickly
        capture web content and save it to ITONICS Cloud. Some users use the Web
        Clipper every day — especially those in executive roles — as an essential
        way to add content into the system.
      </p>
      <p>
        With the current Web Clipper, a user clicks the clipper icon in the
        browser and a window appears where the page's title, description, and
        image are captured. They edit the information if needed and hit save. The
        content is uploaded to the Suite platform, where the clip becomes a post
        in their feed. The extension is available for Chrome, Internet Explorer,
        and Firefox.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <Figure
      src="/images/itonics-web-clipper/current.jpg"
      caption="How the current Web Clipper works"
    />
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="02" title="Problems, goals & users">
    <Prose>
      <h3>Problems & goals</h3>
      <p>
        The clipping process requires a lot of manual work, including specifying
        the data storage destination for each entry, which is time-consuming and
        inefficient. We want to improve the clipping process so that we increase
        app usage and user engagement.
      </p>
      <h3>Users</h3>
      <p>
        The primary users are content creators, content managers, or similar
        executive roles, responsible for creating, editing, and publishing
        content on the platform. The secondary users are project managers and
        employees within the client's organisation — normally not as active as
        the primary users.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="03" title="User research">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        To learn how users use the clipper and understand their pain points, I
        conducted user interviews involving three internal and two external
        users. I gathered qualitative data and analysed their motivations and
        expectations, and their behaviours regarding collaboration. Key findings:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Users want an easy and quick clipping process</li>
        <li>Clear indication of the clipping result can avoid errors</li>
        <li>
          Users want an uncomplicated way to share content while maintaining
          privacy
        </li>
        <li>Users find it difficult to organise content when clipping in high volume</li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="04" title="Ideation — stakeholder workshop">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        I facilitated a design workshop with the key stakeholders — including the
        CTO, a consultant, a content manager, the technical lead, and the product
        manager. The "How might we" (HMW) technique allowed us to reframe insights
        into opportunity areas. Some examples of our HMW statements:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>How might we give users confidence during the clipping process?</li>
        <li>How might we make the clipping process more swift — or more fun?</li>
        <li>How might we provide a quick way to share the clips?</li>
        <li>
          How might we let users with different permissions access the clips?
        </li>
        <li>How might we leverage clip status to enable sharing between users?</li>
      </ul>
      <p>We used dot voting to prioritise our ideas and make decisions.</p>
    </Prose>
    <div class="my-8 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/itonics-web-clipper/workshop-1.jpg"
          alt="Stakeholder workshop"
          loading="lazy"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl bg-card shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
        />
        <figcaption
          class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
        >
          Stakeholder workshop
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/images/itonics-web-clipper/workshop-2.jpg"
          alt="Dot voting and prioritisation"
          loading="lazy"
          class="w-full rounded-2xl bg-card shadow-[0_18px_50px_-24px_rgba(16,24,40,0.28)]"
        />
        <figcaption
          class="mt-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted"
        >
          Dot voting & prioritisation
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src="/images/itonics-web-clipper/ideation.png"
      caption="Crazy-8s ideation sketches"
    />
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="05" title="Constraints & design suggestions">
    <Prose>
      <h3>Known constraints</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>The clip quality relies heavily on the web page structure</li>
        <li>Clipped but unsaved data is lost when the browser session ends</li>
        <li>A file storage destination is required for every clipping</li>
        <li>Extension updates may be difficult with some browsers</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Design suggestions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Clear navigation and interaction that give users better orientation</li>
        <li>Clip more content from the webpage, gaining flexibility for later editing</li>
        <li>
          Remember the user's previous action as a pre-fill, saving time on
          tedious configuration
        </li>
        <li>Optimise the workflow by introducing bulk actions</li>
        <li>Extensive functions to help users deal with a massive number of entries</li>
      </ul>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="06" title="Design deliverables">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        <strong>User task flow.</strong> With the decisions made during the
        workshop and the constraints in mind, I drafted a task flow (happy path)
        to illustrate the user's primary steps to accomplish the clipping process.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <Figure
      src="/images/itonics-web-clipper/task-flow.png"
      caption="User task flow — the happy path"
    />
    <Prose>
      <p>
        <strong>Wireframe.</strong> In the wireframing stage, I defined how the
        Web Clipper page should look. I focused on two challenges: the position of
        the configuration panel required to save clips, and how to help users
        quickly navigate through the clips they want to edit.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <Figure
      src="/images/itonics-web-clipper/wireframe.png"
      caption="Wireframe explorations"
    />
    <Prose>
      <p>
        <strong>Deliverables.</strong> After deciding on the general layout and
        interaction approach, I added visual attributes and detailed interactions
        to the design.
      </p>
    </Prose>
    <Figure
      src="/images/itonics-web-clipper/mockup.png"
      caption="Low- to high-fidelity prototype"
    />
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection num="07" title="Validation">
    <Prose>
      <p>
        We tested usability with five users to evaluate the improved clipper
        workflow. Some insights from the results:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>4 of 5 users found the clipping process straightforward and comprehensible</li>
        <li>
          3 of 5 users found the "Publish" and "Add as Draft" features helpful
          when sharing clips with teammates
        </li>
        <li>
          3 of 5 users were unsure whether clips would remain accessible after
          closing the browser
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The improved Web Clipper received positive feedback from the team and
        users. It is currently in the implementation stage, aiming to release on
        Chrome and Firefox. As a next step, I'd like to further explore ways to
        minimise the number of actions a user must take throughout the clipping
        process.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseStudy>
